# SABOTAGE: WS-NOTES R9 — A STALE BLOCK WRITE IS APPLIED LAST-WRITE-WINS
# (ADR-053 §6 R9; WS-Notes Phase 2 unit 2.2 / gate P2). `Samen.Web.Notes.Writes.cas_update/4`
# no longer compare-and-sets on the revision the writer saw: the filter admits every revision
# AT OR ABOVE it, so a client holding revision 1 when the row is already at 2 matches the
# UPDATE, overwrites the other writer's text, and gets `{:ok, _}` back instead of
# `{:error, :stale}`. Both consequences the design forbids follow: the edit is silently lost
# AND the conflict banner has nothing to show.
#
# Aimed at the CAS, not at the rest of the write path: the transaction boundary, the
# `StaleRecord` -> `:stale` classification in `stale_error?/1`, the block revision bump, and
# every ordering/rebalance clause are untouched. `expected` stays referenced (no unused-variable
# warning) and the masked/unknown-type paths do not change, so only the two R9 tests flip:
# the plain stale write AND the stale split (which must roll the whole transaction back).
# APP: samen_web
# TEST_FILES: test/samen/web/notes_reads_writes_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: REFUSES a stale revision and leaves the row unchanged
# MUST_FAIL: a stale revision refuses the split
diff --git a/samen_web/lib/samen/web/notes/writes.ex b/samen_web/lib/samen/web/notes/writes.ex
--- a/samen_web/lib/samen/web/notes/writes.ex
+++ b/samen_web/lib/samen/web/notes/writes.ex
@@ -506,8 +506,11 @@
   defp cas_update(block, scope, attrs, expected) do
     block
     |> Ash.Changeset.for_update(:update, attrs, scope: scope)
-    |> Ash.Changeset.filter({:revision, [eq: expected]})
+    # SABOTAGE (R9): the compare-and-set now admits every revision AT OR ABOVE the one the
+    # writer saw — a stale write matches the UPDATE and is applied last-write-wins. `expected`
+    # stays referenced, so no warning is introduced.
+    |> Ash.Changeset.filter({:revision, [greater_than: expected - 1]})
     |> update()
     |> case do
       {:ok, updated} -> {:ok, updated}
       {:error, error} -> if stale_error?(error), do: {:error, :stale}, else: {:error, error}
